WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.325
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.849 --> 00:00:07.890
MARC CRANDALL: Hi, folks.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.840
My name is Marc
Crandall, and I handle

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.390
global regulatory compliance
with Google Cloud.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:14.830
A little bit of background.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:16.200
I'm a lawyer by training.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.930
I'm part of Google
Cloud engineering

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.630
to help ensure that the
products we provide to customers

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:26.250
meet their regulatory
compliance needs as best we can.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.150
We focus on three areas.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.320
The first thing is working with
the sales organization as well

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.530
as our legal teams to find
out what the latest trends are

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.560
from a compliance perspective
and what the market needs.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.720
We work with engineering
and product management

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.880
to drive change within
the organization

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.230
to help address those needs.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.960
And finally, we work with
regulatory authorities

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.600
to help learn what the
trends are from regulators

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.780
and also get their input when it
comes to building our services.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.160
I've been at Google 12
years and as a lawyer,

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.770
helped launch one of Google
Cloud's first product

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.830
offerings, G Suite, in 2006.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.180
So let's move forward.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.450
So the thing we want to
talk about is, how can we

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.420
manage regulatory risk?

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:10.950
And when I talk about
managing regulatory risk,

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.830
many folks operate
in regulated markets.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.740
You might operate in
the health care field,

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.530
or in government, or
in financial services,

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:20.940
or education.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.680
Or you might have
privacy considerations,

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.680
such as the recently implemented
General Data Protection

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.040
Regulation GDPR, which
took effect in May.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.260
So a lot of folks have
regulatory compliance

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.370
considerations.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:38.530
So the question is, how
to mitigate that risk.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.840
Now, your counseling--
your compliance experts

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.360
are the folks you
want to look at,

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.800
but we also have experience
in cloud at Google,

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.314
and we'd like to share
some of our experiences.

00:01:50.314 --> 00:01:52.230
So we're going to talk
about four areas today.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.479
We're going to talk about
the importance of conducting

00:01:54.479 --> 00:01:57.360
a compliance assessment
within your own organization,

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.690
your own vertical.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:01.500
We're going to talk about the
importance of transparency

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.160
about the services we provide,
the importance of contracts

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.740
and making sure things are in
writing to demonstrate that

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.020
to your regulators, and
then, time permitting,

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.810
we're going to talk about some
regulatory frameworks that

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:16.140
have been important-- we've
seen in the last few years

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.280
in our work in the cloud.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:22.290
So let's focus first on
compliance assessments.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:26.100
So Google operates in
virtually every country.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.380
Not every country, but almost.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.870
And let's think about all
the industry verticals

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.630
per country, like health
care, education, government,

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.080
manufacturing.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:38.010
There is potentially millions
of regulatory compliance

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.410
combinations worldwide.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.440
At Google we are not the expert
in every single compliance

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.110
framework combination
that's possible.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.960
Our customers and their
industry are the experts

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.240
in their specific field.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:52.170
You have your counsel.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.630
You have your compliance
experts, your chief privacy

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.260
officers, your
compliance officers,

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.780
your security officers.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.180
So you should
leverage what you know

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.830
regarding your specific field.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:05.390
And by the way, your counsel
really can help you there.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.420
What we're saying here
is not legal advice.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.640
You have your own lawyers.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.870
However, we at Google
are experts in the cloud.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.090
And we are also
experts in having

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:19.540
provided cloud services to many
customers who are regulated.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.910
So we have a lot
experience in that regard.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.600
So you can partner with
us-- the work, leverage

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.760
that we have in the
industry with regards

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.170
to helping people, our
experience, our capabilities.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.150
And the point,
then, is that this

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.460
is a shared responsibility.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.110
You have your
compliance obligations.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.180
You have your
compliance expertise.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.992
We have expertise in the cloud.

00:03:40.992 --> 00:03:42.450
And the idea is
that we provide you

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.090
with the transparency
you need to be

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:48.450
able to conduct a meaningful
risk assessment based

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:52.010
on the services we provide.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.730
So when I talk about
meaningful risk assessment,

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:58.790
the key here is to
"think like a regulator."

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.580
In all the years that we've
been doing this with regards

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.510
to Google Cloud,
there's one thing

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:07.790
that's been a common denominator
across all regulated fields

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:08.930
that we've seen.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:12.650
And that common denominator
is that regulators expect--

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.250
they expect that you conduct
your due diligence before you

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:18.350
adopt a cloud provider.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.230
It doesn't matter if it's
the privacy sector in Europe,

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:23.420
or health care in
the United States,

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:27.562
or financial services in
the Asia-Pacific region.

00:04:27.562 --> 00:04:28.520
That's what we've seen.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.110
Regulatory authorities
expect that you

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:31.790
conduct that due diligence.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:33.410
So look at some of these areas.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:35.930
They expect you to know the
answers to these questions

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:38.510
before you adopt cloud.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.490
For example, what are
the security capabilities

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:43.180
of the cloud provider?

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.690
Obviously, you're going to be
seeing a lot of presentations

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.820
this week about our capabilities
from a security perspective.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:51.280
But you need to be able
to answer these questions.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:52.830
Who owns the data?

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:54.810
When you upload your
data to Google Cloud,

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.240
our position is that any data
you upload into the cloud

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.200
remains yours.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.860
It's your data.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:03.600
But again, regulators expect
you to know that answer.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:05.940
How is data being used?

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.630
Google Cloud's position is that
any data that you provide to us

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:12.030
will only be processed
according to your instructions

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:15.275
to provide the services
and to protect that data.

00:05:15.275 --> 00:05:17.910
And the reason why-- how
the data is being used is

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:20.740
so important is because
there are many sectors,

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:24.480
particularly education, or
privacy, or health care,

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:28.170
where data cannot be used for
any other purpose than what is

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:31.230
otherwise required or
prescribed by those regulators.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:35.070
So educational information can't
be used for any other purpose

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:37.110
than for educational purposes.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:41.070
Or in HIPAA, for example,
PHI, which we'll talk about.

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:43.870
Health care information can't
be used for any other purpose

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:46.510
than to provide
health care services.

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:50.430
So that's another thing
regulators expect you to ask.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.200
Data incident notification.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.940
We'll talk about that
a little bit later,

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.460
but heaven forbid
there's a data incident.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.460
You need to know if it's
going to happen or not,

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.680
because you may have your
own compliance obligations

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:02.910
with respect to that.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:05.430
Regulators want to make
sure you know that as well.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.660
Data deletion.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:10.360
How do you know that once you've
deleted data from the cloud,

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:13.120
it's actually gone?

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:14.620
Regulators want you to know.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:16.440
We provide an
obligation ourselves,

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:19.030
a contractual commitment
to purge the day that you

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:21.010
delete it, so you know that.

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:22.570
Data portability.

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:25.030
What happens if you
want to take your data

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:27.650
and move to a
different provider?

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:31.056
Regulators want to know, can
you take the data with you?

00:06:31.056 --> 00:06:32.930
And so you want to look
into the capabilities

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:35.870
that we do provide
for data portability.

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.630
"What type of data
will be stored?"

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:41.600
The type of data is also very
important with regards to

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:43.660
whether or not it is
regulated by a regulator.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:49.080
For example, European privacy
law applies to personal data.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.060
Educational records apply to
educational data, of course.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:57.840
So not all requirements
may apply to all the data

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.075
you may be using the Cloud for.

00:07:00.075 --> 00:07:01.700
So you want to look
at the type of data

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:04.550
you're processing to determine
what regulations might apply

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:05.570
to it.

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:07.280
Of course, "Where is it stored?"

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.530
We've seen regulators
ask customers

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:12.740
to ensure they know what data
centers will be storing data,

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:14.330
or what data centers
may potentially

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:15.680
be storing the data.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.110
And then third-party
audits and certifications.

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:21.660
Is anyone verifying what
we're saying is true?

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:24.170
The one thing I should point
out, and I think a lot of folks

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:25.740
here already know
this, is that there

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.480
is a distinction between Google
Cloud services and our consumer

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:30.470
services.

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:33.680
We do not serve ads on
our Google Cloud products.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:35.119
This is not a consumer offering.

00:07:35.119 --> 00:07:37.160
This is not like Google
Search, where you see ads

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.080
based on your search results.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:39.860
There are no ads.

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:41.735
There are special
business-to-business terms,

00:07:41.735 --> 00:07:43.070
which I'll talk about.

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.720
We have special
administrator tools,

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.750
because our customers are
administering the services, not

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.340
Google directly.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.130
And of course, we have certain
enterprise services like Vault

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:53.600
for G Suite, which
lets you store

00:07:53.600 --> 00:08:00.239
offline copies of the data used
for storage discovery purposes.

00:08:00.239 --> 00:08:02.030
So we talked about
compliance assessments--

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:04.340
the things you need to look at.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:08.150
Now, let's focus
on transparency.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:13.220
So everything that you
could be hearing this week--

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:17.760
everything that I'm saying
to you could be lies.

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:19.850
How do you know we're
not lying to you?

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:22.840
Regulators expect you to know
that what we're saying to you

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:24.230
is true.

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:26.110
And how do you verify that?

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:30.400
The answer is third-party
audits and certifications.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:32.530
What that means
is that we invite

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:35.320
third-party, independent
auditors to come

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:38.080
to Google and audit
our data protection

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:42.039
practices, essentially to
verify our affirmations of what

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:45.200
we do are true.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:48.940
So what does that mean from
an operational perspective?

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:51.790
Let's talk about SOC 2 audits.

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:55.240
So for example, a third-party
auditor will come to Google

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:57.920
and assess each of
our security controls.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.220
So we say that we
do X, we do Y, we

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:04.780
do Z. The auditor comes in
pursuant to a SOC 2 audit.

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:07.150
And they verify each
one of these controls--

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:08.110
dozens and dozens.

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:10.330
I think hundreds of controls.

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:12.310
And the output of that
third-party auditor's

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:16.120
assessment is a highly
confidential, detailed audit

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:17.500
report.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:19.990
The idea, then, from a
regulatory compliance

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:23.930
perspective as part of your due
diligence is to get this report

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:28.960
and then compare it against your
own regulatory, and security,

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:30.910
and data protection needs.

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:31.900
You compare the two.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:34.060
You do a Delta
Analysis to determine

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:37.450
if there are any gaps and
then what those gaps are.

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:40.175
So that's for example,
a SOC 2 report.

00:09:40.175 --> 00:09:41.800
I'm not going to talk
about everything,

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:43.840
but I want to highlight some.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:46.000
Now, the SOC 2 report is--

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.100
as I mentioned, where
auditors come in

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:51.760
and audit us against a
certain auditing standard.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:54.530
But SOC 2 is not a
security standard.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:57.100
And it's not a data
protection standard.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:59.240
So what we decided to
do a few years later--

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.750
we did this starting in 2012--

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:05.020
is that we adopted ISO 27001.

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:10.620
Now, ISO 27001 is an
IT security standard,

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:12.840
where the International
Organization

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:16.110
for Standardization
creates a set of controls.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:17.640
They write the controls.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:20.790
And basically, they say that
if an auditor assesses you

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:23.010
and you've achieved
these controls,

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:28.200
then you have attained
this IT security standard.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:31.260
So the output of that is
essentially a certification.

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:33.810
It's like a diploma,
if you will.

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:36.960
So we have adopted ISO 27001.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:40.350
But the thing about ISO
27001 is that it's not geared

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.060
towards cloud, specifically.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.670
It was drafted originally
a long time ago

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:47.287
before cloud computing
had really taken hold.

00:10:47.287 --> 00:10:48.870
So what the International
Organization

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:52.560
for Standardization did is that
they launched specific cloud

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:58.170
security controls, which is
ISO 27017, which we've adopted,

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:01.740
as well as specific cloud
data protection and privacy

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:05.430
controls, which is ISO 27018.

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:08.970
And again, the auditors audit
us against that standard.

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.250
And if we attain that
standard, which we have,

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:13.210
then we get a certification.

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:15.810
So as part of your
due diligence,

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:17.520
you look at the SOC 2 report.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:19.540
We also have a publicly
available SOC 3.

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:22.520
And by the way, the SOC 2
is NDA, but it's accessible.

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:25.590
We have a publicly
available published SOC 3,

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.560
and then you look at
the ISO certifications.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:30.630
Now, the nice thing
about ISO is that it's

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:33.350
an international organization.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.550
There are participants from
virtually every country

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:36.180
in the world.

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:40.570
And they are the ones who
write the standards themselves.

00:11:40.570 --> 00:11:42.660
In other words, every
country has an opportunity

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.586
to create this global standard.

00:11:44.586 --> 00:11:46.710
And the reason why it's
important from a compliance

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:49.890
perspective is that because
it's an international standard,

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:52.530
it makes it more readily
or easily adoptable

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:54.960
by customers across the world.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:57.390
And it also makes it easier
for regulatory authorities

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:00.780
to recognize the importance
of and the validity

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:03.390
of these standards
and certifications.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:08.550
You'll also see sector-specific
areas such as PCI DSS, which

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:12.330
we attain for processing
payments for some of our cloud

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:13.710
services.

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.300
Privacy Shield,
which is something

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.330
related to European law, which
we'll talk about in a minute.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:22.500
FedRAMP for US
government, MTCS, which

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:24.760
is helpful for
financial services,

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:27.670
particularly banks in Singapore.

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.490
So we'll talk more about
various frameworks.

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:33.580
This is why certifications
are so important.

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:35.790
As a matter of fact, I
can think of a story.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:39.270
There was an
educational institution

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:42.407
in Northern Europe
a few years ago

00:12:42.407 --> 00:12:44.490
that were-- they were using
G Suite for education.

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:46.073
And their regulator
sent them a letter

00:12:46.073 --> 00:12:50.100
and said, how do you know that
Google is not using the student

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:52.530
data for serving ads?

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:55.360
Now, how do you actually
know as the customer?

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:58.260
So the customer went
through the ISO controls.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:12:59.850
They went through the SOC 2.

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:04.650
And they found that there was
a specific control in ISO 27018

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:06.450
that were audited
against, that says

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:08.910
data will not be used for
ads without permission,

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:09.900
without disclosure.

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:12.790
There is a limitation on
how data could be processed.

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:14.740
Remember I mentioned
that earlier?

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:18.150
And they were able to respond to
the regulatory inquiry, citing

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:21.360
the certification that
we have in ISO 27018

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:24.092
and the controls that are
part of that certification.

00:13:24.092 --> 00:13:26.550
And you can get the controls
directly from ISO, by the way.

00:13:29.266 --> 00:13:31.140
And this is an example
of some of the control

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:33.360
of some of the objectives
and some of the controls

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:35.057
that are reviewed as
part of this audit.

00:13:35.057 --> 00:13:36.640
I'm not going to go
through every one.

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:38.010
We don't have enough time.

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:42.420
But some examples-- information
security, purpose and scope

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:43.170
of processing.

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:45.503
Remember I talked about how
regulators want to make sure

00:13:45.503 --> 00:13:47.470
that processing is limited?

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:49.500
This is where that
would be covered.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.900
Data minimization,
collection limitations,

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:53.970
not collecting or
using more data than

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:58.140
is otherwise required to provide
the services, communications

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:03.150
security, business continuity,
cryptography, access controls.

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:05.437
A lot of very important,
interesting areas

00:14:05.437 --> 00:14:07.020
that you might want
to take a look at.

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:13.270
In addition to-- again, as I
mentioned the due diligence

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:16.060
that regulators
expect you to conduct,

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:18.940
there are also online
resources that are available.

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:20.920
We create white
papers periodically

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:24.070
to help you in your
assessment of our services.

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:26.260
I'm now going to talk
about it in a little while.

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:28.990
But we have recently launched
a GDPR Resource Center

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:32.560
that helps you assess
our services with respect

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:35.680
to the European General
Data Protection Regulation.

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:39.160
We have white papers information
about our data centers,

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:42.020
security and
compliance, encryption.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:44.770
This is great, because you have
an opportunity to review these.

00:14:44.770 --> 00:14:46.875
And then when your
regulator asks,

00:14:46.875 --> 00:14:48.250
have you done your
due diligence?

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:50.349
You can say, I've looked
at these white papers.

00:14:50.349 --> 00:14:51.640
I've looked at this collateral.

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:53.110
I've looked at the SOC 2 report.

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:55.180
I've looked at the
ISO certification.

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:57.000
I obtained the controls
from ISO and have

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:00.050
reviewed those, compared them
against our requirements.

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:02.220
And there's a lineup
and they match.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:07.490
Another interesting
aspect is data centers.

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:09.600
Regulators have
often asked, do you

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:11.590
know where the data
is being stored?

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:14.940
Now, some regulatory
frameworks specifically

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:17.490
require you to know
where data is located.

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:19.800
And some of our services,
actually within Google Cloud,

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:22.130
allow you to locate
data in specific areas--

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:24.450
a copy of data in
specific areas.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:28.650
But they may ask,
for example, why

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:32.860
has Google chosen to put data
centers in particular regions?

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:35.526
So let's talk about
that for a minute.

00:15:35.526 --> 00:15:38.520
We choose to place data
centers in certain regions,

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:41.910
as people might imagine,
for bandwidth reasons,

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:44.224
for cost, for energy savings.

00:15:44.224 --> 00:15:46.140
But then, of course,
there are policy and rule

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:47.550
of law considerations.

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:50.220
We won't build a data
center in any location

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:52.440
where there is
not a rule of law,

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:54.682
where there is no
stable government.

00:15:54.682 --> 00:15:56.640
We want to make sure that
there are protections

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:00.660
against content, copyright,
or trademark concerns

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:01.860
for a provider.

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:03.330
And we can only
build data centers

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:05.670
and will only build data
centers where we are allowed

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:08.160
to do so as a US-based company.

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:10.960
You won't see data centers
in OFAC-restricted countries,

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:11.480
for example.

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:17.070
So we've talked about the
importance of conducting

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:20.130
compliance assessments
for your sector,

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:22.650
with your counsel, your
compliance professionals,

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:25.800
the importance of transparency,
making sure you conduct

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:28.470
your due diligence, and the
information we can provide

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:29.970
to you to help with that.

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:32.560
Now, let's talk about contracts.

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:36.430
Now, third-party
audits are terrific.

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:38.870
They verify what
we're saying is true.

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:42.460
But it doesn't mean anything,
unless it's put in writing.

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:45.610
And we have seen situations
where financial regulators have

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:49.060
asked our customers for copies
of their cloud contracts

00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:52.630
with their cloud
provider, to verify

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:54.700
that they reflect
the regulatory needs

00:16:54.700 --> 00:16:56.140
of that particular industry.

00:16:56.140 --> 00:16:58.300
We've seen financial
services regulators do this

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:00.590
more than once.

00:17:00.590 --> 00:17:03.594
So let's have a brief
overview of what we provide

00:17:03.594 --> 00:17:05.510
from a contractual
perspective because, again,

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:08.710
these obligations
need to be in writing.

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:11.849
So first, we have the
customer agreement.

00:17:11.849 --> 00:17:15.420
This includes commercial
terms, billing and payments,

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:20.849
how much it's going to cost,
what type of technical support

00:17:20.849 --> 00:17:22.500
there's going to be.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:24.210
Who owns the
intellectual property?

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:26.220
As I mentioned, you
own the data that you

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:28.040
upload into Google Cloud.

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:30.870
Those cover the general
commercial terms.

00:17:30.870 --> 00:17:32.340
But then, we also
have something--

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:34.530
and I'm going to focus on
this in the next slide,

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:35.460
particularly.

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:38.370
We have something called
data processing terms.

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:40.200
This is a special part
of the contract that

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:44.280
sets forth the data protection
obligations that we have

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:47.460
with respect to the protection
of our Google Cloud customers'

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:48.294
data.

00:17:48.294 --> 00:17:49.710
This is something
that we actually

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:50.880
did not have originally.

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:53.760
We had this incorporated
into our earlier contracts.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:56.430
But having a special
section, a special amendment

00:17:56.430 --> 00:18:00.120
for data protection is something
that we implemented in 2012,

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:02.220
based actually on
guidance from data

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:04.360
protection regulators in Europe.

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:07.260
This includes things like
the privacy commitments.

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:08.790
Remember that data
use restriction?

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:11.310
We won't use your data for
ads, or for any other purpose

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:14.070
than per your instruction
or provide the services.

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:15.030
That's where it is.

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:17.410
It's in the data
processing amendment.

00:18:17.410 --> 00:18:20.502
A commitment to maintain
third-party audits, right?

00:18:20.502 --> 00:18:22.710
We want to keep doing this
and be able to provide you

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:24.090
with these certifications.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:27.050
An obligation to delete
the data, for example.

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:29.750
I'll go into more detail of
the data processing terms

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:31.810
in the next slide,
as I mentioned.

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:35.400
But also, a cloud
contract isn't necessarily

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:38.850
appropriate for every
country for every vertical.

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:41.160
So for certain verticals,
for certain countries,

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:44.370
for certain areas we have
special industry-specific

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:46.380
terms, special
amendments that would

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:49.210
be appropriate for
that particular region.

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:51.570
For example, financial services.

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:54.210
A regulatory authority
have special requirements

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:56.310
and expect their
banks, their securities

00:18:56.310 --> 00:18:58.680
firms to be able
to have terms that

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:00.660
address those requirements.

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:05.520
Business Associates Agreements,
which is important for HIPAA.

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:10.740
That is US-based protection
of patient records.

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:13.170
As I mentioned,
financial services terms.

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:16.590
EU Model Clauses, which
is used to transfer

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:19.680
personal data from Europe
to the rest of the world.

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:24.430
And FedRAMP, which is
for US government data.

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:27.060
So let's talk about the
data processing terms,

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:29.320
which I mentioned earlier.

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:32.370
They are the data protection
obligations that we provide.

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:34.379
And from a regulatory
compliance perspective,

00:19:34.379 --> 00:19:36.420
this is something that's
going to be particularly

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:38.490
important when it
comes to working

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:40.080
with regulatory authorities.

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:42.660
It is essentially
the privacy policy

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:45.090
for Google Cloud contracts.

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:46.860
And it was something
that, as I mentioned,

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:49.920
we initially launched in 2012.

00:19:49.920 --> 00:19:51.750
But it's been evolving
over the years.

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:53.340
It's not a static document.

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:55.140
We are continually
getting feedback

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:58.170
from regulatory authorities,
particularly in Europe

00:19:58.170 --> 00:20:01.112
and in regulated markets
like financial services.

00:20:01.112 --> 00:20:02.820
And then we're updating
it over the years

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:04.740
to reflect latest requirements.

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:09.930
As a matter of fact, it was
most recently updated for GDPR.

00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:14.210
But we did this in 2017 to be
ready for when GDPR takes--

00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:16.967
or took effect in May 2018.

00:20:16.967 --> 00:20:19.050
And incidentally, if you
are existing Google Cloud

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:22.020
customers, you want to
make sure that you opt in

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:27.140
and use the latest data
processing terms for GDPR.

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:30.790
Well, let's talk about some
of the areas within GDPR--

00:20:30.790 --> 00:20:33.580
or specifically within
the data processing terms.

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:34.780
Excuse me.

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:37.600
This is where we include the
limitation on processing,

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:38.560
as I mentioned before.

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:40.532
We won't use the data
for any other purpose

00:20:40.532 --> 00:20:41.740
than to provide the services.

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:43.480
There are no ads.

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:44.530
Data deletion.

00:20:44.530 --> 00:20:46.180
This is where we
include the obligation

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:48.760
that when you
delete data, it will

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:51.200
be purged within a
specific period of time.

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:53.110
It's not going to just
sit there forever.

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:55.480
And I would venture that
this is better than what

00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:58.460
a lot of people do on on print.

00:20:58.460 --> 00:21:00.260
This is where we
include the obligation

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:04.040
to maintain these third-party
audits and certifications.

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:05.510
Because after all,
what's the point

00:21:05.510 --> 00:21:08.960
if-- as soon as you sign
up, we no longer do it?

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:11.960
So this is why here we include
an obligation to constantly

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:14.450
and continually do it
throughout the entire term

00:21:14.450 --> 00:21:16.060
of your contract.

00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:19.200
Subprocessors-- we haven't
talked about this before.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:22.326
What if Google uses
vendors or third parties?

00:21:22.326 --> 00:21:24.200
The regulatory authorities
that you work with

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:26.900
will want to know, is
Google using any vendors?

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:29.190
Are they sharing
data with anyone?

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:31.460
Well, from a Google
perspective, we

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:33.930
do the vast majority of
processing ourselves,

00:21:33.930 --> 00:21:35.750
of course, because we're Google.

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:37.520
But we do have some vendors.

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:39.170
And so here we
include an obligation

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:42.170
to make sure that we let you
know who those vendors are.

00:21:42.170 --> 00:21:43.790
We provide you
with notice in case

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:47.930
we decide to use a new vendor,
that they comply with certain--

00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:50.697
very stringent
security requirements.

00:21:50.697 --> 00:21:52.280
And the vendors we
use, even though we

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:54.230
do the vast majority of
processing, generally

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:56.600
would be things like
technical support,

00:21:56.600 --> 00:22:02.330
providing 24/7 customer
service, 24-hour access,

00:22:02.330 --> 00:22:04.010
different language support.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:04.940
So they are there.

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:07.570
And this is where
you can find them.

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:09.090
An obligation to
protect your data.

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:11.840
The security measures
that we'll use.

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:14.360
Remember I told you about
data incident notification?

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:17.340
That's something
else that's critical.

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:18.470
I hope it never happens.

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:20.750
But let's say that there
is a data incident that

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:21.540
affects your data.

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:23.390
There's a breach, for example.

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:26.780
Many regulatory
authorities demand

00:22:26.780 --> 00:22:29.270
that you have obligations
to your own customers,

00:22:29.270 --> 00:22:31.910
to your own users if
there is a breach.

00:22:31.910 --> 00:22:33.980
But how can you
fulfill that obligation

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:38.450
if your cloud provider doesn't
tell you if it happens to them?

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:39.710
So we include an obligation.

00:22:39.710 --> 00:22:41.793
And we've actually been
doing this for many years,

00:22:41.793 --> 00:22:45.080
probably from the first days
of Google Cloud G Suite, where

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:48.110
we provide you with notification
if there's an incident that

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:49.900
affects your data.

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:51.820
And of course,
the other thing is

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:54.380
that we make available a data
privacy officer that folks

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:57.640
can contact, if they have
any questions that affect

00:22:57.640 --> 00:22:59.670
European privacy, for example.

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:04.130
So that's why data processing
terms are so important.

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:07.120
So we've talked about
compliance assessments.

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:10.000
We've talked about
transparency, contracts.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:13.652
And now, let's talk a little
bit about regulatory frameworks.

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:20.520
I want to touch upon finance,
because financial services is

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:21.370
a very big deal.

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:25.729
There's been a huge adoption
by banks of cloud services.

00:23:25.729 --> 00:23:27.270
So let's talk a
little bit about what

00:23:27.270 --> 00:23:29.810
regulators in the
financial services space

00:23:29.810 --> 00:23:32.710
have been asking questions
about in our experience.

00:23:32.710 --> 00:23:34.380
So as I mentioned,
all the areas we

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:36.090
discussed regarding
the due diligence

00:23:36.090 --> 00:23:37.890
questions at the beginning--

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:38.760
who owns the data?

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:42.960
Where is it stored per
processing limitations?

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:46.230
But then regulators in the
financial services space

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:50.220
also maintain very strict
oversight over their banks.

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:53.460
Obviously, it's a highly,
highly regulated industry.

00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:57.000
So regulatory authorities
want to make sure that when

00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:59.790
a financial services customer
moves all of their data

00:23:59.790 --> 00:24:03.090
into the cloud, that
the regulator still

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:05.670
maintains the ability
to exercise oversight

00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:07.470
over that data.

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:10.050
Financial services
regulators expect

00:24:10.050 --> 00:24:13.380
that a bank will be able to
subject themselves to an audit

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:17.142
by the regulatory authority
at the regulator's choosing.

00:24:17.142 --> 00:24:19.600
And they want to make sure that
if the cloud customer moves

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:21.530
their data to the
cloud, they can do so.

00:24:21.530 --> 00:24:24.000
So in our financial
services terms,

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:26.880
we include language that
says we acknowledge you.

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:29.100
Customers, the
bank are regulated

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:32.470
by your regulatory authority.

00:24:32.470 --> 00:24:36.150
And that you will, as such,
pursuant to your obligations,

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:38.500
make that data available
to the regulator

00:24:38.500 --> 00:24:40.470
if they request
an audit from you,

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:42.270
and that they still
have jurisdiction.

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:46.200
Regulators want to see that type
of language with the customer,

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:48.660
so that customers can
verify to the regulators

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:51.870
that they can still be subjected
to audits, for example.

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:53.070
But it goes beyond that.

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:56.090
Some regulatory authorities in
the financial services space

00:24:56.090 --> 00:24:59.490
want to be able to regulate
us, or audit us directly

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:00.560
as the provider.

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:02.550
So we've had to
address that and agree

00:25:02.550 --> 00:25:06.410
to these sort of requirements
for certain jurisdictions.

00:25:06.410 --> 00:25:08.300
Same thing with customers.

00:25:08.300 --> 00:25:10.100
In many different
markets, customers

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:12.980
are expected to be able
to conduct audits directly

00:25:12.980 --> 00:25:14.090
over Google.

00:25:14.090 --> 00:25:16.320
And we agree to this
in many jurisdictions.

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:18.440
And naturally, we
much prefer the use

00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:21.830
of third-party audit reports
and certifications, because it's

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:23.900
much, much more scalable.

00:25:23.900 --> 00:25:26.210
But in situations
where that is an issue,

00:25:26.210 --> 00:25:27.710
we still agree to
it, because that's

00:25:27.710 --> 00:25:32.460
what many financial services
regulators have expected of us.

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:36.670
Another area that we address--
so that's financial services.

00:25:36.670 --> 00:25:40.470
Another area that we look at
and we provide services for

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:43.280
would be health care
in the US, for example.

00:25:43.280 --> 00:25:45.780
The Health Insurance Portability
and Accountability Act.

00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:47.700
You've heard me mention HIPAA.

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:49.620
We do provide
services to entities

00:25:49.620 --> 00:25:52.500
that are regulated by HIPAA,
that are covered by HIPAA.

00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:56.700
HIPAA involves protection of
protected health information.

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:58.110
And to address
this, for example,

00:25:58.110 --> 00:26:00.975
we offer what's called
Business Associates Agreements.

00:26:00.975 --> 00:26:03.390
You remember when I was
talking about contracts where

00:26:03.390 --> 00:26:06.390
we had three different slides--

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:09.120
where we talked about the main
commercial contract, the data

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:12.330
processing terms, and
sector-specific terms?

00:26:12.330 --> 00:26:14.220
The Business
Associates Agreement

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:17.190
would be a great example
of sector-specific terms.

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:19.260
We also have special
implementation guides,

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:21.240
like the HIPAA
Implementation Guide,

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:22.950
where you can review
it and determine

00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:25.410
how best to deploy Google
Cloud services when

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:27.480
it comes to protection of data.

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:29.370
This is, again,
white paper resources

00:26:29.370 --> 00:26:32.020
that I mentioned earlier.

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:35.130
In providing services to
the educational market,

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:39.030
FERPA, for example, is data that
involves student records that

00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:39.870
must be protected.

00:26:39.870 --> 00:26:41.700
So we have special
language that addresses

00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:44.030
that in the education space.

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:47.070
And then there are other
markets like government.

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:48.960
For example, in the
US, as I mentioned--

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:53.070
you heard me mention FedRAMP
off of the FedRAMP authorization

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:55.710
to operate.

00:26:55.710 --> 00:26:57.120
We're at moderate level.

00:26:57.120 --> 00:27:00.060
And that means certain
government agencies can store

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:02.610
their data in Google Cloud.

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:06.840
United Kingdom-- the NCSC
and the participation

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:09.030
in the G-Cloud
marketplace to help

00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:11.070
government entities
in the United Kingdom

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:13.080
use Google Cloud.

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:17.850
And again, you heard me mention
that ISO certification is

00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:21.270
helpful globally in the
vast majority of markets.

00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:25.230
In many cases, a lot of these
programs are related to ISO.

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:27.720
And we can expect that
in some governments,

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:30.610
they have special
requirements such as FedRAMP.

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:36.540
Just an example of some of the
financial services companies

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:40.290
that have adopted Google
Cloud over the years.

00:27:40.290 --> 00:27:42.270
Again, these are
highly regulated,

00:27:42.270 --> 00:27:44.880
yet we have been able to
create an offering which

00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:47.340
helps the customers address
their regulatory compliance

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:48.600
needs in the cloud.

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:51.840
So we've been very successful
in being able to do this.

00:27:51.840 --> 00:27:52.990
It's a lot of work.

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:58.080
But again, this is a developing
area and very exciting.

00:27:58.080 --> 00:28:00.270
Now, I can't talk about
regulatory frameworks

00:28:00.270 --> 00:28:03.330
without talking about
European privacy.

00:28:03.330 --> 00:28:06.360
So let's talk a
little bit about that.

00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:07.800
Now, this involves
the protection

00:28:07.800 --> 00:28:11.490
of personal data of
European citizens--

00:28:11.490 --> 00:28:13.240
European users.

00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:16.960
So let's start at least from
the beginning of my discussion.

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:19.290
This really started,
as far as we're

00:28:19.290 --> 00:28:23.320
involved, with the European Data
Protection Directive of 1995.

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:25.770
This implemented very
strict privacy controls

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:29.520
over personal data of Europeans.

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:33.180
And this basically
set the stage for what

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:37.080
Google Cloud and a lot of
our cloud provider peers

00:28:37.080 --> 00:28:39.640
would focus on when
creating our services,

00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:42.390
particularly when it comes to
business-to-business services.

00:28:42.390 --> 00:28:45.180
One very important restriction
of the EU Data Protection

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:48.570
Directive of 1995
was that it limited

00:28:48.570 --> 00:28:51.360
the export or the
transfer of personal data

00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:54.960
from Europe to any other country
in the world that was not

00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:58.920
considered to have adequate
privacy protections as

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:01.710
determined by Europe.

00:29:01.710 --> 00:29:04.400
Now, to help address this
data transfer issue--

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:06.900
and you have to remember, when
the Data Protection Directive

00:29:06.900 --> 00:29:09.540
was passed, the internet
wasn't really being

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:11.520
used for commerce, right?

00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:13.050
There's a huge shift.

00:29:13.050 --> 00:29:16.440
So Safe Harbor
was passed in 2000

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:20.250
that would allow the transfer
of personal data to the US.

00:29:20.250 --> 00:29:24.190
How many people know now that
Safe Harbor was invalidated?

00:29:24.190 --> 00:29:26.540
There we go.

00:29:26.540 --> 00:29:29.350
And then in 2012,
because many countries

00:29:29.350 --> 00:29:32.350
were concerned that they
couldn't receive personal data,

00:29:32.350 --> 00:29:35.710
the European Commission
launched Model Clauses.

00:29:35.710 --> 00:29:38.350
And what that is, is these
are special contractual terms,

00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:42.190
where if you enter into these
with your cloud provider,

00:29:42.190 --> 00:29:45.420
data could be transferred
anywhere in the world,

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:47.720
assuming that both the
customer and the cloud provider

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:50.000
comply with the requirements.

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:53.960
And then later in
2016, the EU and the US

00:29:53.960 --> 00:29:56.510
created Privacy Shield,
which is then, again,

00:29:56.510 --> 00:29:59.570
another framework that
replaces Safe Harbor to allow

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:02.870
the transfer of personal
data to the United States.

00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:06.200
So the net-net of this is
that even though the European

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:09.140
privacy legislation limits the
transfer of personal data from

00:30:09.140 --> 00:30:12.140
Europe, there are regulatory
frameworks that Google has

00:30:12.140 --> 00:30:13.040
adopted--

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:14.881
Privacy Shield and
Model Clauses--

00:30:14.881 --> 00:30:17.255
that allow data to flow through
our global infrastructure

00:30:17.255 --> 00:30:18.870
of data centers.

00:30:18.870 --> 00:30:22.430
Now, let's turn to what's most
important to a lot of people--

00:30:22.430 --> 00:30:24.530
the recently passed
General Data--

00:30:24.530 --> 00:30:27.770
or the recently enacted General
Data Protection Regulation,

00:30:27.770 --> 00:30:34.300
GDPR, which replaces
the 1995 directive.

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:36.730
The GDPR, of course, is
extremely significant.

00:30:36.730 --> 00:30:38.590
It's the most important
piece of legislation

00:30:38.590 --> 00:30:40.450
since the 1995 directive.

00:30:40.450 --> 00:30:42.620
And the idea is that
it's a regulation.

00:30:42.620 --> 00:30:44.920
It's not a directive.

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:45.910
What's the difference?

00:30:45.910 --> 00:30:48.940
The directive from
1995 basically

00:30:48.940 --> 00:30:51.610
directed member states
in Europe to pass

00:30:51.610 --> 00:30:54.310
their own laws that
reflected the requirements

00:30:54.310 --> 00:30:55.840
of the directive.

00:30:55.840 --> 00:31:00.160
The result was similar laws,
but somewhat of a patchwork,

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:02.890
which made compliance
challenging for providers

00:31:02.890 --> 00:31:04.150
and customers alike.

00:31:04.150 --> 00:31:05.680
So this is a regulation.

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:08.380
It has direct force
of effective law,

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:10.450
presumably to help
reduce the likelihood

00:31:10.450 --> 00:31:14.500
of inconsistent enforcement
or inconsistent laws.

00:31:14.500 --> 00:31:17.540
And it strengthens the
rights of individuals.

00:31:17.540 --> 00:31:20.350
So what does that mean as
far as what the General Data

00:31:20.350 --> 00:31:22.990
Protection Regulation requires?

00:31:22.990 --> 00:31:25.660
You'll see a lot of things
here look familiar to what

00:31:25.660 --> 00:31:27.692
we've talked about before.

00:31:27.692 --> 00:31:29.780
So the core principles
of processing

00:31:29.780 --> 00:31:33.050
remain the same as what the
Data Protection Directive had.

00:31:33.050 --> 00:31:34.510
Purposeful limitation.

00:31:34.510 --> 00:31:37.130
The data will only
be used and processed

00:31:37.130 --> 00:31:38.990
based on instructions
from customers

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:41.810
and for a particular purpose.

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:44.250
Commitments on data deletion.

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:48.680
So when data is deleted,
it's actually deleted.

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:51.410
Security commitments,
international data transfers.

00:31:51.410 --> 00:31:54.350
You remember you heard me
talk about Privacy Shield

00:31:54.350 --> 00:31:56.060
and Model Clauses
is very important

00:31:56.060 --> 00:31:58.760
to allow the flow of
data around the world?

00:31:58.760 --> 00:32:00.590
Those graded data
transfer mechanisms

00:32:00.590 --> 00:32:03.530
are still recognized
and valid under GDPR.

00:32:03.530 --> 00:32:06.190
Very important.

00:32:06.190 --> 00:32:08.570
There are stricter obligations
for data processors.

00:32:08.570 --> 00:32:11.540
I should mention that customers
under the European model

00:32:11.540 --> 00:32:13.640
are typically data controllers.

00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:15.950
They decide how the
data is processed.

00:32:15.950 --> 00:32:19.310
And Google Cloud as the cloud
provider is the data processor.

00:32:19.310 --> 00:32:21.350
We follow the instructions
of our customers

00:32:21.350 --> 00:32:23.940
only when it comes to
the processing of data.

00:32:23.940 --> 00:32:28.220
So there are new, stricter
obligations in GDPR

00:32:28.220 --> 00:32:30.980
than there were in the
Data Protection Directive.

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:33.830
And this is based on,
actually, in a lot

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:35.900
of guidance the data
protection regulators have

00:32:35.900 --> 00:32:37.310
issued over the years.

00:32:37.310 --> 00:32:41.840
That guidance was in turn
incorporated into GDPR.

00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:45.440
But we've been doing it for many
years, so it's not new for us.

00:32:45.440 --> 00:32:47.194
You remember incident
notification,

00:32:47.194 --> 00:32:48.610
where we indicated
that we provide

00:32:48.610 --> 00:32:51.380
a commitment to let you know
if there's a data incident?

00:32:51.380 --> 00:32:53.262
That's now part of GDPR.

00:32:53.262 --> 00:32:54.970
We've been doing it
for many, many years,

00:32:54.970 --> 00:32:56.450
but it's now a requirement.

00:32:56.450 --> 00:32:57.640
Data return.

00:32:57.640 --> 00:32:59.170
There's now a
requirement in GDPR

00:32:59.170 --> 00:33:02.810
that there is the data
portability capability which,

00:33:02.810 --> 00:33:05.180
as I mentioned to you, we
have as part of our services

00:33:05.180 --> 00:33:07.791
and in our contracts.

00:33:07.791 --> 00:33:10.040
There is an obligation that
data protection regulators

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:12.080
and customers have
an audit right, which

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:15.650
we've also maintained
for many, many years now.

00:33:15.650 --> 00:33:17.500
And brand new aspects.

00:33:17.500 --> 00:33:19.370
There are greater fines.

00:33:19.370 --> 00:33:21.860
You've probably heard a
lot about GDPR in the news,

00:33:21.860 --> 00:33:24.590
because the fines
for non-compliance

00:33:24.590 --> 00:33:27.990
can be significant--
very, very high.

00:33:27.990 --> 00:33:31.700
Also, GDPR applies directly
to providers like Google.

00:33:31.700 --> 00:33:33.080
It's not just the
customer that's

00:33:33.080 --> 00:33:35.870
responsible for compliance,
but now under GDPR,

00:33:35.870 --> 00:33:39.800
the providers are responsible
for compliance as well.

00:33:39.800 --> 00:33:40.940
Records of processing.

00:33:40.940 --> 00:33:44.270
You have to maintain information
about yourself, and who

00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:47.480
is actually processing the
data, and who's responsible,

00:33:47.480 --> 00:33:49.610
and detailed contractual
provisions that

00:33:49.610 --> 00:33:51.540
address GDPR requirements.

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:53.270
You remember I mentioned
earlier that we

00:33:53.270 --> 00:33:55.580
want to make sure that
if you have already

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:57.710
opted into using
Google Cloud, that you

00:33:57.710 --> 00:33:59.180
update your data
processing terms

00:33:59.180 --> 00:34:00.986
to reflect GDPR commitments?

00:34:00.986 --> 00:34:02.110
This is one of the reasons.

00:34:02.110 --> 00:34:04.640
You want to make sure that the
terms you have are compliant.

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:06.330
We have that available online.

00:34:06.330 --> 00:34:08.989
You can do a Google Search
and opt in if you need to.

00:34:11.500 --> 00:34:14.050
As far as where
customers should start--

00:34:14.050 --> 00:34:16.900
of course, you should
familiarize yourself with GDPR.

00:34:16.900 --> 00:34:18.489
You should seek
counsel yourself.

00:34:18.489 --> 00:34:19.489
We are not your lawyers.

00:34:19.489 --> 00:34:21.800
This is not legal advice.

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:24.219
Know the type of data
that you're collecting.

00:34:24.219 --> 00:34:26.110
As the customer, you're
a data controller.

00:34:26.110 --> 00:34:27.190
So you want to
make sure that you

00:34:27.190 --> 00:34:29.469
know what you're collecting,
why you're collecting it

00:34:29.469 --> 00:34:31.540
from your employees
or your customers,

00:34:31.540 --> 00:34:33.340
the reasons you're
collecting it, making

00:34:33.340 --> 00:34:35.980
sure you don't retain it
for longer than you have to.

00:34:35.980 --> 00:34:40.090
You want to review your
current controls and any gaps.

00:34:40.090 --> 00:34:42.940
You want to make sure that you
leverage the data protection

00:34:42.940 --> 00:34:44.830
features of Google Cloud.

00:34:44.830 --> 00:34:47.920
You remember I mentioned that--

00:34:47.920 --> 00:34:50.409
we talk about our security
controls and our SOC 2

00:34:50.409 --> 00:34:53.110
report, the white papers.

00:34:53.110 --> 00:34:54.639
What's available
publicly online?

00:34:54.639 --> 00:34:57.310
Leverage that for
GDPR compliance.

00:34:57.310 --> 00:35:01.120
And of course, GDPR is evolving.

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:04.780
The law has passed, but data
protection authority guidance

00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:06.130
continues.

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:09.010
And so you want to make
sure you monitor any updates

00:35:09.010 --> 00:35:12.010
to what this may
bring in the future,

00:35:12.010 --> 00:35:16.180
as regulators issue
ongoing advice.

00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:18.940
And finally, we have
a GDPR Resource Center

00:35:18.940 --> 00:35:21.010
that is also very
important when it

00:35:21.010 --> 00:35:24.850
comes to ensuring that you have
conducted your due diligence

00:35:24.850 --> 00:35:26.320
when adopting Google Cloud.

00:35:26.320 --> 00:35:30.310
We talk about contracts
and terms, compliance,

00:35:30.310 --> 00:35:34.630
compliance analysis, and
relevant products and services

00:35:34.630 --> 00:35:37.270
that you can leverage to help
with your GDPR compliance

00:35:37.270 --> 00:35:38.890
effort.

00:35:38.890 --> 00:35:41.085
So that's very important.

00:35:41.085 --> 00:35:43.335
The other thing I mentioned
was regulatory engagement.

00:35:43.335 --> 00:35:45.610
It's important to
stay a step ahead.

00:35:45.610 --> 00:35:48.600
So from our perspective
as a cloud provider,

00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:51.420
we've worked very closely with
data protection authorities,

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:54.240
financial services
regulators around the world

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:56.970
to ensure that the services
that we are providing--

00:35:56.970 --> 00:35:59.190
to help ensure the
services we are providing

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:02.730
are consistent with regulatory
requirements worldwide.

00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:05.070
That may involve educating
regulatory authorities

00:36:05.070 --> 00:36:09.030
about the cloud, but also,
monitoring whether or not

00:36:09.030 --> 00:36:12.240
they are issuing guidance
regarding cloud services

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:13.290
adoption.

00:36:13.290 --> 00:36:15.840
And also, being able
to take feedback

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:18.720
from the regulatory
authority folks

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:21.720
and then incorporate that into
our products and services,

00:36:21.720 --> 00:36:26.390
like I mentioned at the
beginning of our program.

00:36:26.390 --> 00:36:27.220
So let's summarize.

00:36:27.220 --> 00:36:29.890
We talked about the importance
of conducting a compliance

00:36:29.890 --> 00:36:33.880
assessment in your particular
field and your vertical.

00:36:33.880 --> 00:36:37.300
We talked about the questions
regulators expect you to ask.

00:36:37.300 --> 00:36:40.540
We talked about transparency,
the importance of making sure

00:36:40.540 --> 00:36:42.070
you have the
information you need,

00:36:42.070 --> 00:36:44.110
so that you can conduct
a meaningful risk

00:36:44.110 --> 00:36:48.460
assessment to address what
regulators want you to do.

00:36:48.460 --> 00:36:50.050
Contracts-- the
importance of making

00:36:50.050 --> 00:36:53.364
sure you have things in
